# agents

The agent loop and prompt surface. Single source of truth for prompt
strings; the registry pattern keeps variant cells coupled to runtime.

## Must
- All mono prompt strings live in `src/luxe/agents/prompts.py` `PROMPT_REGISTRY`
- `TaskOverlay` maps `task_type` to `PromptVariant` id; routing goes through `resolve_prompt_ids`
- `run_agent` (loop.py) owns the inner tool-call loop; `single.py` is the entry
- `run_agent`'s mutable per-run state lives in ONE record, `LoopState`
  (`agents/loopstate.py`, 2026-09): same field names and initial values the
  bare locals had. Configuration read once from `RunFlags` and per-step
  scratch values stay locals. `loop.py` remains the sole mutator; the counter
  discipline below applies to the fields unchanged
- Every `events.jsonl` record `run_agent` writes goes through `emit(kind,
  **fields)` (`loop._event_emitter`), which owns the `log_calls` gate and the
  leading `phase=` field, so field names AND order match what
  `scripts/toolcall_taxonomy.py` / `outcomes.py` parse. `emit` resolves
  `append_event` from `luxe.agents.loop` at call time (tests monkeypatch it
  there). No bare `append_event(` / `if log_calls:` in the body —
  `tests/test_loop_telemetry.py` pins it
- `tests/test_golden_trajectory.py` pins ten scripted multi-step trajectories
  byte-for-byte (every request body, every events record in order, the
  AgentResult). A change to what the model sees or what telemetry records
  must regenerate a snapshot deliberately, in the same commit as its cause
- `guardrails.py` is the SOLE home of intervention thresholds + nudge bodies
  (`_WRITE_PRESSURE_*`, `_ACTION_DENSITY_GATE_*`,
  `_EARLY_BAIL_*`, `_HABITUATION_EXIT_*`, `_POST_WRITE_IDLE_MAX`,
  `_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_REPEAT_STEPS`, `_BREADTH_PROBE_ESCALATION_COUNT`,
  `_CONVERGENCE_{LOW,HIGH}_THRESHOLD`, `_v1105_synthesis_looping_signature`,
  and since 2026-09 the loop-authored `_DEDUP_MESSAGE_TEMPLATE` /
  `dedup_message` and `_SPEC_ZERO_CALLS_DECLINE_MESSAGE`, plus the
  action-density gate's convergence predicates
  `ActionDensityGateGuard.convergence_suppression` / `.suppressed_converged`)
  together with their calibration rationale. `loop.py` RE-EXPORTS them so the
  loop test modules' `from luxe.agents.loop import _X` keeps resolving;
  `tests/test_guardrails_identity.py` pins `loop.X is guardrails.X`. Never
  reintroduce a local definition — the 2026-05-26 extraction copied instead of
  moving and the two sets silently coexisted until 2026-08-04
- Mode B write-pressure injection (v1.4.1) is opt-in via `LUXE_WRITE_PRESSURE=1`
- Every `LUXE_*` switch `run_agent` obeys is read in `agents/flags.py`
  (`RunFlags.from_env`), once, at the top of the run — NOT with an
  `os.environ.get` in the body. `run_agent` assigns each field to the local
  name it always used, so the extraction changed no behaviour: same defaults,
  same "only the exact string 1" / "only the exact string 0" asymmetries, same
  silent fallback for every malformed value. A new switch adds a field there
  plus a row in `tests/test_run_flags.py`, and a test asserts the loop's body
  contains no `os.environ` access. Three reads deliberately stay outside:
  `LUXE_TOKEN_LOG_INTERVAL` (loop.py module constant, read at import),
  `LUXE_LOAD_PRIORS` (cohort_priors) and `LUXE_EARLY_BAIL_TRAJECTORY_SHAPE`
  (guardrails) — those belong to their own modules' contracts.
  `LUXE_EARLY_BAIL_MODE` is NOT one of them any more: `EarlyBailGuard` used to
  re-read it from os.environ up to three times per step; since 2026-09 it is
  `RunFlags.early_bail_mode` (verbatim string, same `"default"` default,
  exported-but-empty stays `""`) passed in as `early_bail_mode=`
- SpecDD Lever 2 chain block (`_build_sdd_block`) is appended after the variant's `task_prefix`

### Reflection / verify stage invariants (Track 1, pinned 2026-05-24)

- **reflect surface ownership**: `reflect.py` is the single source of truth for the
  verifier/critique prompt surface (the `_VERIFIER_SYSTEM` + per-driver templates). This
  is DISTINCT from `PROMPT_REGISTRY` (task-completion variants) by design — the verifier
  is a separate "second-look" critic, not a `run_agent` task prompt. New verifier prompt
  wording still requires a test in `tests/test_prompts.py` (the registry rule's intent).
- **opt-in + disable-equivalence**: the stage is gated by `LUXE_REFLECT` (default unset →
  off). With it off, generation is byte-identical (the primitive is pure; call sites gate).
- **verify-only is non-perturbing**: a verify call is a SEPARATE `backend.chat`; in
  verify-only mode it never mutates the work being judged. So Phase 1 can be measured
  offline by replaying it over stored transcripts without changing any benchmark verdict.
- **`gap` requires a substantiated FUNCTIONAL BLOCKER**: `gap=true` only when the model
  both asserts it AND supplies ≥1 deficiency; stylistic/suboptimal observations never flip
  it (defends the false-gap metric). Unparseable verdicts fail CLOSED (gap=false).
- **deterministic, no persistence**: temp=0; reflect must not read or write cross-run state.

### Phase 2 repair invariants (multi_turn; pinned 2026-05-24)

- **two-gate fire (give-up signature AND verify gap)**: the repair stage fires only when
  a turn produced ZERO tool calls (`adapter._is_giveup_turn` — the empty_turn give-up
  signature) AND a separate verify call returns `gap=true`. The structural gate goes
  FIRST — it gates the expensive verify call and, by construction, skips the verifier's
  reporting-gap false-gaps (which have non-empty action sets). A verify call is never
  made on a turn that already acted.
- **locked repair budget**: exactly ONE corrective re-prompt per flagged turn (a single
  `_luxe_repair`-marked user nudge + one bounded step pass over the SAME exposed tool
  surface). Hard stop: no re-verify, no second repair, no loop. The re-prompt's step loop is
  capped at `_REPAIR_MAX_STEPS` (=4) — TIGHTER than the initial turn's `_MAX_STEPS_PER_TURN`
  (=15) — to bound the over-action/runaway mode (the Phase-2 `miss_func_112` 6-step/51-call
  spiral); `_MAX_CALLS_PER_PROBLEM` still backstops the whole problem. The cap is
  artifact-scoped (covers every observed Phase-2 repair in the A/B archive), not re-benched.
- **same tool surface**: the repair re-prompt is driven with the turn's already-computed
  `openai_tools` (the per-turn exposed surface) — repair may NOT widen the surface or
  invoke tools outside the original trajectory's surface (pre-registered budget).
- **append-to-same-turn**: repair output extends `decoded_turns[-1]` (the give-up turn),
  never a new turn entry — turn count must stay aligned with the GT turn count.
- **generic nudge**: `reflect.repair_nudge` carries no benchmark semantics; it re-states
  the request as unfinished and lists the verifier's deficiencies (consuming the verdict,
  not re-solving). The `_luxe_repair` marker keeps the nudge out of later verify contexts.

### forge-hybrid Phase 2 (A) compaction invariants (pinned 2026-05-28, DEFAULT-ON)

- **TieredCompact ships default-ON** (`LUXE_TIERED_COMPACT` defaults to enabled; set to "0"
  to disable for ablation). The 4-arm n=75 sweep (OFF / 0.75 / 0.50 / 0.40) + 4-arm
  n=14 stress + 2-rep noise validation at phase_thresholds=(0.50, 0.85, 0.95) jointly
  established: resolve rate is equivalent to no-compaction baseline within substrate
  noise band (±2.8 at n=75); wall improvement is 42-56% (well outside substrate wall
  noise ~20%); 2 protected wrong_target instances (matplotlib-25775, pylint-6528) heal
  via phase-1 fires; zero new wrong_target damages. This is the cycle's only Pareto-
  positive lever.
- **Default phase_thresholds = (0.50, 0.85, 0.95)** owned by
  `luxe.context.TieredCompact._DEFAULT_PHASE_THRESHOLDS`. Aggressive phase 1 (50% pressure)
  captures protective phase-1 wins (drop _luxe_nudge + truncate tool_results); conservative
  phase 3 (95% pressure) avoids the destructive reasoning-drop mode (observed at 1/75
  firing rate at this threshold). The phase-1-helps / phase-3-hurts split is the cycle's
  load-bearing design finding.
- **Strict counter discipline**: `writes_seen`, `reads`, `tool_calls_total`, and all
  `*_fired` flags are explicit integer/boolean state owned by `run_agent`. They are
  NEVER recomputed by scanning `messages`. Compaction can drop arbitrary message-level
  content from the eligible region; loop state must stay consistent under compaction.
  This is a regression guard against any future "lazy fix" that derives counters from
  message inspection.
- **Marker-based nudge identification**: TieredCompact identifies droppable nudges
  via the `_luxe_nudge` (and `_luxe_repair`) keys on message dicts, NOT by body-text
  matching. Body-text matching is FORBIDDEN — it's fragile under message-text drift.
  Every guardrail that injects a nudge MUST tag the message with `_luxe_nudge=True` and
  `_luxe_nudge_type="<guard_name>"`. See `docs/luxe-markers-audit.md`.
- **Protected messages**: `messages[0]` (system prompt) and `messages[1]` (original
  task) are NEVER eligible for compaction. The last `keep_recent` assistant-message
  iterations are also protected. Compaction operates only on messages indexed
  `[2, eligible_end)`.
  This is LOAD-BEARING and is not to be relaxed to reach an oversized message:
  when fewer than `keep_recent` assistant messages exist `_find_eligible_end`
  returns 2 and every phase is a no-op, which is correct here and is why the
  2026-08-24 single-result bound lives at CREATION instead — see "Single
  tool-result clamp" below.

### Server-truth context calibration (pinned 2026-08-11, DEFAULT-ON, UNBENCHED)

- `LUXE_CTX_SERVER_TRUTH` (`RunFlags.ctx_server_truth`, default ON; only the
  exact string "0" disables, the tiered_compact spelling). After each response
  the loop computes `calibration_ratio(resp.timing.prompt_tokens, est_sent)`
  and folds it into the DENOMINATOR via `calibrated_ctx_limit` — every
  consumer (`context_pressure`, `TieredCompact.compact`,
  `elide_old_tool_results`) divides by `effective_ctx` and inherits the
  correction without learning about it. **The phase thresholds keep their
  pinned (0.50, 0.85, 0.95) values** — this changes what the fraction MEANS,
  never the fraction.
- Why: `estimate_tokens` is `len(text) // 4`, which runs far low on the code
  and JSON tool payloads an agent loop actually carries. Measured 2026-08-11:
  1.84x / 1.92x / 2.24x across three turns of session 0e524f033300, and
  **2.38x-3.69x** through a live 13-step log-analysis run (a 576 KB file read
  in windows), where `est=19.8%` against a true `47.3%`. So the phases were
  firing somewhere near 1.0-1.9x of the real window — phases 2 and 3 could not
  fire at all before the server rejected the prompt outright. The status bar
  was corrected to server truth in June (`last_prompt_tokens / num_ctx`); the
  thresholds were not, and the two have disagreed by 2x+ ever since.
- The ratio is NOT a constant to hardcode: it drifts within a run (3.69x at
  step 1 down to 2.38x by step 13 as prose displaces the tool schemas the
  estimate misses hardest) and varies by workload. That is why it is
  re-measured every step rather than latched or tabulated.
- Step 1 is always uncalibrated (nothing to calibrate against) and matches the
  historical reading exactly. Recalibrated EVERY step rather than latched: the
  prose/code/JSON mix shifts as tool payloads accumulate.
- `calibration_ratio` degrades to 1.0 on a missing or zero `prompt_tokens` —
  a backend reporting no usage falls back to the estimate, never to a wrong
  number — and is clamped to [0.5, 8.0] so one malformed usage block cannot
  swing compaction to an extreme.
- **UNBENCHED, and the specific risk is phase 3.** Measured 2026-08-11 on a
  synthetic 20-step trajectory at num_ctx=32768: the OLD reading gave 37.7%
  pressure and fired **phase 0** (nothing compacted); the same messages at the
  real-world cal=2.4x give 90.6% and fire **phase 2** (tool results dropped,
  all 20 assistant texts preserved); at the 8.0x clamp ceiling they give 302%
  and fire **phase 3**, which drops 17 of 20 assistant reasoning messages.
  Phase 3 is the mode `agents.sdd` already records as destructive (it "DESTROY[s]
  existing patches"; the phase-1-helps/phase-3-hurts split is the compaction
  cycle's load-bearing finding), and until now it was effectively DEAD CODE —
  reaching 0.95 on the estimate required ~228% real context, which the server
  rejects first. Calibration makes it reachable for the first time.
- That reads as a hazard and is probably the opposite. A high calibration means
  the prompt genuinely does not fit: at cal=8 an est of 12,368 is ~98,944 real
  tokens against a 32,768 window, so the OLD behaviour was to compact nothing
  and send an overflowing prompt into `validate_context_window`'s HTTP 400 —
  i.e. a **context_overflow bailout**. Phase 3 dropping reasoning is the
  correct response to a context that has actually overflowed; failing the
  request is not. **Testable prediction for the bench run: context_overflow
  bailouts should DECREASE.** (Suggestive prior: C10's own writeup noted "one
  recovered context_overflow bailout" as the only delta between two arms that
  were byte-identical requests — i.e. that recovery was noise, and the class
  exists in this suite.) Do NOT lower `CALIBRATION_MAX` to suppress phase 3
  without first checking that number; the clamp exists to bound a malformed
  usage report, not to keep a legitimate overflow response from firing.
- `LUXE_CTX_SERVER_TRUTH=0` restores the pre-2026-08-11 behaviour exactly; run
  maintain_suite before treating the new default as settled.

### Single tool-result clamp (pinned 2026-08-24, opt-in, UNBENCHED)

- `LUXE_TOOL_RESULT_CLAMP=1` (`RunFlags.tool_result_clamp`) bounds ONE tool
  result at the moment `run_agent` appends it to `messages`. **Default OFF;
  with it unset `clamp_tool_result` is never called and the appended string is
  the untouched `executed.error or executed.result`** — byte-identical by
  construction, pinned by
  `tests/test_loop_context_bounds.py::TestDefaultPathIsUntouched`, which
  compares the full request sequence across the unset case and every near-miss
  spelling (`""`, `"0"`, `"true"`, `"01"`, `" 1"`, `"on"`, …).
- Why it is NOT a `TieredCompact` change. Evidence
  (`acceptance/chat_bigread_2026_08_24/EVIDENCE.md`, session `168f1825a1fd`):
  one step read a 257,988 B file and a 23,775 B file; the next request
  recorded `compaction_phase_reached phase_reached=3 tokens_before=71616
  tokens_after=71616 tool_results_dropped=0` and the turn was lost. The most
  aggressive tier fired and dropped nothing because `_find_eligible_end`
  returns 2 when fewer than `keep_recent=3` assistant messages exist — which
  is every early step of an interactive turn. **The fix is not to relax the
  protected-message invariant above.** `messages[0]`/`messages[1]` and the last
  `keep_recent` assistant iterations stay never-eligible; the bound belongs one
  layer down, at CREATION. `keep_recent`, the pinned `(0.50, 0.85, 0.95)`
  thresholds, and `_find_eligible_end`'s semantics are UNCHANGED by this work.
- **The value is `bash`, `grep`, and MCP tools.** `read_file` already has
  `LUXE_TOOL_BUDGET_CTX` (`tools.sdd`), which makes the clamp largely redundant
  there; nothing else in the tool surface has a budget of any kind.
- Budget: `luxe.context.tool_result_clamp_chars(effective_ctx)` =
  `max(8 KB, effective_ctx * 0.25 * 4)`. The fraction and floor are
  DELIBERATELY the same 0.25 / 8 KB pair as `tools.fs.READ_BUDGET_FRACTION` /
  `READ_BUDGET_FLOOR`, so the two budgets cannot disagree about what one result
  is worth — passed the CALIBRATED limit at cal=2.4x it lands on
  `budget_for_ctx(32768) == 13653` exactly. Counted in CHARACTERS because
  characters are what `estimate_tokens` (chars//4) divides; the `* 4` is that
  function's own inverse, not a second tokenizer guess.
- **Known asymmetry with the Phase 2 read budget, and it cuts the wrong way.**
  This clamp derives its bound from `effective_ctx`, which is uncalibrated at
  step 1 — so the FIRST step's bound is the loosest one the run will ever have
  (at cal=1.0 a 32,768-token window allows 32,768 chars; once the loop learns
  cal≈2.4x the same window allows 13,653). `tools.fs.budget_for_ctx` has no
  such gap: it works from raw `num_ctx` and folds the measured 2.4x in as a
  constant, so its bound is the same on every step. **The incident this lever
  was written for died on an early step**, which is precisely where this lever
  is weakest — the clamp would have fired on `self.md` at 257,988 B either way,
  but a result between 13.6 KB and 32.7 KB slips through at step 1 and would
  not at step 3. Two ways to close it if the bench shows it matters: seed the
  step-1 calibration from `_CHARS_PER_TOKEN`, or take the bound from
  `budget_for_ctx(role_cfg.num_ctx)` instead. Neither is done here — closing it
  by guessing a step-1 ratio is the same error Phase 4 exists to correct, and
  the choice should be made on bench evidence, not symmetry.
- **The trailer must never imply a resume that does not exist.** `read_file`
  output is line-numbered (`f"{i + offset + 1}\t{line}"`), so the offset is
  RECOVERED from the last whole line that survived and the trailer carries the
  same `continue with read_file(path=…, offset=N, limit=N)` shape `tools/fs.py`
  emits. For every other tool — and for `read_file` output with no recoverable
  line number, or no `path` argument — the trailer says what was dropped and
  that re-running will not recover it. A guessed offset would send the model
  round the identical call forever.
- The two other `role: "tool"` appends in the loop (the schema reject and the
  dedup short-circuit) are deliberately NOT clamped: neither runs
  `dispatch_tool`, and both carry loop-authored constants — `validate_args`
  returns a fixed sentence that never echoes an argument value, and the dedup
  message is literal. Clamping them could only ever be a no-op on a hot path.
- `executed.bytes_out`, `ToolCall.result` and the `tool_call` event keep
  reporting what the TOOL produced. The clamp is about what the CONTEXT
  carries; conflating the two would make the corpus lie about tool behaviour.
  What the clamp did is its own record: `tool_result_clamped`
  (name/max_chars/chars_dropped/bytes_out), gated by `log_calls`.
- **UNBENCHED.** Promotion needs `python -m benchmarks.maintain_suite.run`
  3 reps x 10 plus hand-reading every PASS via the local-branch ref
  (bench-as-truth). The specific risk to watch is the one PLAN.md names: a
  budget that turns one fatal turn into three timid ones is not a win — check
  whether the model actually uses the resume offset.

### Calibration extrapolation damping (pinned 2026-08-24, opt-in, UNBENCHED)

- `LUXE_CTX_CAL_DAMP=1` (`RunFlags.ctx_cal_damp`) shrinks the server-truth
  calibration toward the unmeasured-material ratio in proportion to how much of
  the CURRENT prompt the calibration sample actually covered. **Default OFF;
  with it unset `step_calibration is ctx_calibration` at every step**, so
  `effective_ctx`, the `cal=` debug field, and every downstream consumer read
  exactly as they did before — byte-identical, pinned by the same
  `TestDefaultPathIsUntouched` class.
- The problem it addresses (`EVIDENCE.md` finding 3, session `168f1825a1fd`):
  the ratio is measured on the PREVIOUS response. At step 1 that prompt is
  system-prompt-and-tool-JSON, which tokenizes far worse than chars/4 —
  observed step-1 ratios 2.64x / 2.16x / 1.91x / 1.79x. Applied whole to a next
  prompt that is 96% prose, where the SAME sessions recalibrated to 1.27x and
  1.21x, it reported **102.5% pressure on a request that was ~65% of a 128K
  window**, and the failure was then blamed on the endpoint. The section above
  already records the drift; what it did not do is bound how far one sample may
  be extrapolated.
- Mechanism (`luxe.context.damped_calibration`). Real tokens are additive over
  characters, so the honest reading of a prompt whose composition shifted is a
  char-weighted blend::

      share  = est_at_calibration / est_now      (capped at 1.0)
      damped = R_unmeasured + (calibration - R_unmeasured) * share

  `R_unmeasured` defaults to `CALIBRATION_UNMEASURED_RATIO = 1.2`, the MEASURED
  chars/4-undercount of ordinary body text from the corpus above (1.27x and
  1.21x once prose landed), cited the way `tools.fs._CHARS_PER_TOKEN` cites its
  own live measurement. Damping toward 1.0 instead was considered and rejected:
  it under-reads prose by ~17%, which is the pre-2026-08-11 failure in
  miniature.
- **The constant is a free parameter and is SWEEPABLE**:
  `LUXE_CTX_CAL_UNMEASURED_RATIO` (`RunFlags.ctx_cal_unmeasured_ratio`), a
  float on the `LUXE_TIERED_COMPACT_THRESHOLD` grammar — out of the pinned
  `[CALIBRATION_MIN, CALIBRATION_MAX]` band or unparseable degrades SILENTLY to
  1.2, so a sweep script exporting junk costs a default arm rather than a
  crashed run. It reaches `damped_calibration` as a keyword argument, never as
  a module-constant read, so no arm requires a source edit; and it is inert
  while `LUXE_CTX_CAL_DAMP` is unset, so an arm that exports only the ratio is
  a DEFAULT arm and the bench matrix can say so. A knob benched in a form that
  cannot move is the C10 trap (lessons.md 2026-08-11). The safety properties
  above hold at every value in the band, including the one that would otherwise
  inflate an uncalibrated step — tested.
- Two properties make it safe rather than merely plausible, and both are
  tested: (a) **it only ever moves TOWARD 1.0, never past it and never away
  from it** — the blend is clamped to the interval between `calibration` and
  1.0, so an uncalibrated step stays exactly 1.0, a backend reporting no usage
  still degrades to 1.0, and a sub-1.0 ratio cannot be inflated by the
  constant; (b) **the pinned [0.5, 8.0] clamp survives by construction**, since
  the result lies between two values already inside the band.
- **The pinned `(0.50, 0.85, 0.95)` thresholds keep their values.** This
  changes what the fraction MEANS, never the fraction — the same contract the
  server-truth section states. Step 1 stays uncalibrated. The ratio is still
  re-measured every step and never latched; `est_at_calibration` records the
  size of the prompt the live ratio describes and is read only by this lever.
- Gradual growth is barely touched (share≈0.9 keeps ~90% of the correction),
  which is the SWE-bench/maintain trajectory shape; the 40x single-step jump is
  the shape this exists for. Live replay: a 1.88x ratio measured on a
  ~1,650-token prompt, applied to a 71,616-token estimate, damps to 1.22x —
  66% of a 128K window instead of 102.5%, and no longer above the phase-3
  trigger. Pinned in `tests/test_context.py::TestDampedCalibration` and
  `tests/test_loop_context_bounds.py::TestCalibrationDamping`.
- It says out loud what it did: a `ctx calibration damped 1.88x -> 1.22x
  (measured at est=…, prompt now est=…)` debug line, only when the value
  actually moved. A silent correction is how the last one hid for months.
- With `LUXE_CTX_SERVER_TRUTH=0` the calibration never leaves 1.0, so damping
  is inert — the ablation restores the pre-2026-08-11 reading whole regardless
  of this flag.
- **UNBENCHED. Promotion needs 3 reps x 10 maintain_suite against the shipped
  default, SWEEPING the ratio over at least 1.0 / 1.2 / 1.6** so the constant
  is chosen by evidence rather than inherited from one session.
- **The mechanism of the risk, not just its direction.** 1.2 is
  PROSE-DERIVED — two recalibrations in one markdown-heavy session. The
  dangerous case is a composition shift toward the material chars/4 misses
  HARDEST: a large JSON tool result, an MCP payload, a minified bundle, a
  dense source file, where the true unmeasured ratio is nearer 2.4 than 1.2.
  In that shape the blend UNDER-reads, compaction fires late or not at all, and
  the server rejects the prompt outright — the exact `context_overflow` failure
  the server-truth section was written to eliminate, and whose count that
  section names as its own testable prediction. So the direction of risk is the
  OPPOSITE of that section's, and **the number to check is the same one:
  `context_overflow` bailouts must not increase on any swept arm.** A 1.6 arm
  that beats 1.2 on that count is evidence the constant is prose-biased.
- **What to check it on, and what maintain_suite does NOT have.** The fixture
  to watch is one whose SINGLE-STEP growth is JSON or code rather than prose.
  The suite has partial coverage: `isomer-implement-healthcheck` targets
  `app.py` at 45,886 B of Python, the `nothing-ever-happens-*` pair sits over a
  75,538 B `bot/strategy/nothing_happens.py`, and `isomer/data/*.json` holds
  41,926 B and 89,921 B of JSON — all readable in one call, all landing as
  roughly 4-6x single-step growth over a maintain step-1 prompt at
  `num_ctx: 32768`. **The incident's MAGNITUDE is not represented**: nothing in
  the suite produces the ~40x single-step jump that made the extrapolation
  error visible, and the one file large enough to (`the-game`'s 316,061 B
  `previous-versions/movies_storage.json`) is above `read_limit()` (262,144),
  so `read_file` refuses it and it never lands as a single result at all. Treat
  that as a FINDING the promotion decision must weigh, not a gap to leave
  implicit: a green 3x10 bounds the lever's harm at 4-6x growth on code, and
  says nothing about it at 40x. Evidence at the incident magnitude has to come
  from a chat-side drill (`acceptance/chat_bigread_2026_08_24/`), the way
  Phase 2's did.

### Tool schemas in the calibration (pinned 2026-10-20, default-ON same day)

- `LUXE_CTX_CAL_TOOLS` (`RunFlags.ctx_cal_tools`) adds
  `estimate_tools_tokens(openai_tools)` — the request's tool schemas, chars//4,
  computed once per run — to the estimate the server-truth ratio divides
  (`est_sent`, and the damping lever's `est_now`), and subtracts the same
  amount from `effective_ctx`. **Default ON; only the exact string `"0"`
  disables it**, and disabled the overhead is 0 and every number is the
  pre-2026-10-20 reading exactly.
- Why (`acceptance/harness_ab_2026_10/REPORT.md` § Glimmer neon-reset): the
  server counts the schemas in `usage.prompt_tokens` on every call;
  `estimate_messages_tokens` never saw them. On a short early prompt they
  dominate, so the ratio measured ~7x at step 0 (2,225 / 320) and 2.83x at
  step 4, was multiplied onto a 34 KB file read, and fired phase 2 at 36% of
  the real window, dropping the file one step after it was read. The damping
  lever above treats the symptom (extrapolation); this removes the cause
  (an estimate missing a fixed part of the prompt).
- The window charge is what keeps the quotient honest: consumers divide the
  MESSAGE estimate by `effective_ctx`, so the reading is the message share of
  what the schemas leave, and at 1.0 messages + schemas fill the server's
  window exactly. The pinned `(0.50, 0.85, 0.95)` thresholds keep their
  values. With `LUXE_CTX_SERVER_TRUTH=0` the ratio stays 1.0 but the schemas
  still come off the window — they are real prompt either way. A run with no
  tools is unchanged. Pinned in
  `tests/test_loop_context_bounds.py::TestToolSchemasInCalibration`.
- It can change WHEN compaction fires for every model. Champion no-harm 10x3
  on m5, same day, interleaved with a shipped-defaults control
  (`acceptance/ctx_cal_tools_2026_10/REPORT.md`): 30/30 both arms; 9/10
  fixtures sent byte-identical requests and produced the identical diff in all
  six runs, compaction firing at the same steps; the tenth
  (document-config) varies within the control arm alone. Reported peak
  pressure drops (e.g. 0.57 -> 0.22 on a short doc task) without moving any
  compaction decision. Promoted on no-harm: for the champion it is a
  correction of the measurement, inert on outcome. Glimmer neon-reset with it
  alone: 1/3 REAL (0/4 before) — one of three conditions, as traced.
- The golden trajectories were regenerated at the flip: their fake server
  counts the whole request body, tools included, against 2.5–8K toy windows,
  so the charge moves pressure everywhere and compaction timing in
  `default_flags`/`clamp_and_damp`. `TestCalibrationDamping` pins `=0`: its
  replayed ratio is the 2026-08-24 one, measured without the schemas.

### forge-hybrid Phase 4 (D) trajectory-shape invariants (pinned 2026-05-28, opt-in)

- **`LUXE_EARLY_BAIL_TRAJECTORY_SHAPE` defaults OFF.** The D smoke at n=14 confirmed
  the locked predicate (`sustained_low_trend ≥ 3 AND grep_vs_read_ratio < 0.5 AND
  breadth_saturation < 0.6`) is too narrow for this champion at num_ctx=32768: 0/14
  suppression fires across the smoke set. The lever is in-tree as infrastructure but
  the predicate does not engage; future cycles may trace-mine to find empirically-grounded
  thresholds.
- **Implicit dependency on `LUXE_ADAPTIVE_POLICY=1`** for `score_log` population. With
  adaptive policy off, the predicate is a no-op (sustained_low_trend = 0). This is a
  documented coupling, not a bug.

### Tool-call telemetry events (pinned 2026-08-04, additive-only)

- `tool_reject` (reason=schema|unknown_tool, name + message) and
  `textfallback_drop` (names + recovered) land in events.jsonl, gated by
  `log_calls` like every other event. They are TELEMETRY: they must never
  add/remove/alter a `messages` entry, change dispatch, or gate control flow.
  The 2026-08 taxonomy refuted every hardening candidate, so observation
  shipped without behavior change — keep it that way unless a future
  evidence-cleared cycle says otherwise.
- `_parse_text_tool_calls(drops=)` collects dropped candidate names
  (deduplicated — the `<tool_call>` block and the bare-JSON scanner see the
  same candidate); the default `drops=None` call is byte-identical to the
  pre-2026-08-04 signature.
- `scripts/toolcall_taxonomy.py` prefers these direct events and suppresses
  its legacy proxies per-call/per-run; if either event's fields change,
  update the script's suppression paths in the same commit.
- `compaction_phase_reached` gained `effective` and `eligible_end`, and
  `compaction_phase_at_resolve` gained `ineffective_fires` (2026-08-24, NO
  flag, same additive-only contract). Existing field names and values are
  untouched. `CompactionResult.effective` is a derived PROPERTY, not a stored
  field, so it cannot drift from the counters it reads and adding it changed no
  constructor call; `eligible_end` is a defaulted field (`None` = no phase
  ran). Why it exists: a `phase_reached=3` line reads as the most aggressive
  tier responding to pressure, but the record that lost session
  `168f1825a1fd`'s turn also carried `tokens_before == tokens_after == 71616`
  and zero drops. Telemetry that cannot say "this achieved nothing" is how that
  hid for months. **Checked before landing**: no events.jsonl consumer reads
  compaction records at all — `scripts/*.py` (taxonomy included),
  `benchmarks/maintain_suite/run.py` and the test corpus were swept, so there
  was no field-shape assumption to break.

### Truncated-turn retry (pinned 2026-08-10, DEFAULT-ON, BENCHED)

- `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=1` (`RunFlags.truncated_turn_retry`): when a turn
  returns `finish_reason == "length"` AND carries no tool call, replay the
  cut-off text into history, append `_TRUNCATED_TURN_MESSAGE`, and CONTINUE
  instead of taking the terminal path. Bounded by
  `_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` (2). **DEFAULT-ON since 2026-08-10** — only
  the exact string "0" disables it, the tiered_compact spelling. Set
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0` for ablation.
- Why: the loop's terminal test is `if not tool_calls:` and it never consulted
  `finish_reason`, so a turn CUT OFF mid-sentence was indistinguishable from a
  model that finished and chose to answer. The run ended `aborted=False` with
  no diff and no gate fired.
- Founding instance: lpe-rope-calc-implement-strict-flag, 6/6 identical runs,
  exactly 8,192 completion tokens (the cap), cut off mid-word, scored 1/5 for
  "produced no diff" while reporting a clean completion. Neither
  write_pressure (needs >=10 tool calls; had 3) nor prose_burst (needs 0; had
  3) can see that shape.
- ONLY `"length"` triggers it. `"stop"` is a model that chose to end, and
  retrying that would change behaviour on every ordinary completion in the
  suite; an unknown reason is not evidence of truncation. A truncated turn that
  DID make a tool call is left alone — it already acted.
- **A/B verdict (2026-08-10): all four pre-registered criteria met.** 3 reps x
  10 fixtures x 2 arms: 27/30 -> 30/30, score 111 -> 120, zero regressions,
  tokens +22.5% / wall +7.8% (ceiling was ~2x), and 3/3 firings landed on
  genuine cap hits with zero spurious fires. `terminal_turn_truncated` went
  3 -> 0 while `truncated_turn_retry` went 0 -> 3; all recovered on retry #1.
  Report: `acceptance/truncated_turn_ab_2026_08_10/REPORT.md`.
- Promoted to default-ON on that evidence (user decision, 2026-08-10). Caveat
  recorded because it governs any future re-tune: the mechanism fired 3 times,
  all on ONE fixture. The no-harm result is broad (27 untouched runs); the
  it-helps result is n=1 fixture, because the suite contains exactly one
  capped-turn `implement` trajectory. **If a workload behaves unexpectedly,
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0` is the first thing to try** — wider evidence
  than one fixture has never been gathered, and SWE-bench is where it would
  come from.
- `terminal_turn_truncated` is UNGATED telemetry (additive-only, per the
  telemetry section above): it fires in both arms and is the only record that
  distinguishes "finished" from "cut off". Keep it ungated whatever the
  default becomes.
- **The bound is configurable** (2026-08-11):
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` -> `RunFlags.truncated_turn_max_retries`,
  default `_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` (2, the benched value). Non-negative
  int; anything malformed degrades silently to the default, like every other
  knob in `flags.py`. Passed to `TruncatedTurnGuard.should_fire(max_retries=)`
  and into the `truncated_turn_retry` event, so the records say what bound was
  in force rather than assuming the constant.
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_MAX_RETRIES=0` is NOT the same as
  `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0`: the mechanism stays enabled, so
  `terminal_turn_truncated` still reports `retry_enabled=True` and an ablation
  stays distinguishable from a tightened leash in the corpus.
  **Why it exists**: each retry is a full capped generation — ~2.5 min at
  8,192 tokens on the champion — and in chat the capped turn is frequently the
  model rambling rather than mid-edit, so the default spends ~8 min before the
  turn ends. Observed in session 0e524f033300 (2026-08-11), the first sighting
  of this mechanism outside the bench. Lowering the bound for chat is a
  judgement call, NOT a benched change: the 30/30 result was measured at 2 and
  the benchmark/maintain path must keep reading the unset default.
- `on_notice` (`run_agent`/`run_single`, default `None`) is the DISPLAY-ONLY
  seam that announces a retry to a front-end. It never influences control
  flow, is never consulted for a decision, and is wrapped so a raising
  callback cannot kill a run. Chat wires it; the benchmark/maintain path
  passes nothing and is unchanged. It exists because the retry was visible
  only in `events.jsonl` — a file nobody reads mid-turn — while it spent
  minutes behind a spinner. The terminal `finish_reason == "length"` case
  notifies too, whatever the switch: a turn reported as an answer when it was
  actually cut off is the exact confusion this whole mechanism was built for.

### Empty-completion retry (pinned 2026-08-17, DEFAULT-ON, UNBENCHED)

- `LUXE_EMPTY_TURN_RETRY` (`RunFlags.empty_turn_retry`, DEFAULT-ON; only the
  exact string "0" disables it, the tiered_compact spelling): when a terminal
  turn carries NO tool call and NO text, append `_EMPTY_TURN_MESSAGE` and
  CONTINUE instead of taking the terminal path. Bounded by
  `_EMPTY_TURN_MAX_RETRIES` (1). Same mechanism, same shape and the same
  reasoning as truncated-turn retry above — it is deliberately modelled on it.
- Why: the terminal test is `if not tool_calls:`, and an empty `resp.text`
  satisfies it exactly as a real answer does. A blank reply was therefore
  recorded as a finished turn — `aborted=False`, steps=1, no gate, no warning,
  and nothing in the record separating "answered" from "said nothing".
- Founding instance: session 0eb5998d8825 turn -5 (2026-08-17), a hosted
  REASONING model (`moonshotai/kimi-k3`): steps=1, tool_calls=0, normal finish
  reason, `content` empty — the model spent its budget in the reasoning
  channel and emitted no content at all. The shape is NOT reasoning-specific
  (any provider hiccup produces it), so the guard keys on the observable
  emptiness, never on the model.
- Whitespace-only counts as empty: a lone newline is not an answer.
  `finish_reason == "length"` is EXCLUDED — that is truncation, which
  `TruncatedTurnGuard` owns with its own message and its own larger budget,
  and it is ordered first so the two never spend both budgets on one response.
- ONE retry. A model that returns nothing twice will not answer on a third,
  and each attempt is a full generation.
- **This reaches the BENCHMARK path, intentionally**, exactly as truncated-turn
  retry did: a bench run that records an empty answer as a completion is
  measuring the wrong thing. **UNBENCHED** — no maintain_suite A/B has been
  run. If a workload behaves unexpectedly, `LUXE_EMPTY_TURN_RETRY=0` joins
  `LUXE_TIERED_COMPACT=0` and `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY=0` as a first thing
  to try.
- `terminal_turn_empty` is UNGATED telemetry (additive-only): it fires in both
  arms and is the only record distinguishing an answer from silence. It
  carries `reasoning_chars`, because "thought for 3,568 characters and said
  nothing" and "returned nothing" are different failures with different fixes.
  Keep it ungated whatever the default becomes.
- `on_notice` announces it live, same seam and same rules as above.

### Intermediate-step prose (`AgentResult.step_texts`, 2026-08-17)

- A step that emits text ALONGSIDE tool calls appends that text to
  `result.step_texts`. Purely additive reporting, like `last_prompt_tokens`:
  `final_text` keeps its exact meaning (the LAST step's text), no loop logic
  reads the new field, and no benchmark grader sees anything new.
- It exists because models routinely put the opening of their answer on an
  acting step ("Let me check the config…", or a whole lead-in paragraph) and
  chat rendered `final_text` alone, so replies began mid-thought (session
  0eb5998d8825 turn -7: 3 steps, 2 tool calls, the lead-in stranded in step 1).
  The JOIN is done in the chat layer only (`render.compose_answer`) — do not
  fold it into `final_text` here, or every grader's input changes.

### Removed mechanisms (2026-09 agent-loop cleanup)

Each was re-verified dead or refuted before deletion; the default-flag
request bytes and `events.jsonl` records are pinned unchanged by
`tests/test_golden_trajectory.py` (recorded on origin/main before the
removal). `outcomes.py` keeps classifying their historical event names so old
logs still parse. Do not reintroduce any of them without new evidence.

- **Prose-burst detector** (`LUXE_PROSE_BURST`, `ProseBurstGuard`,
  `_PROSE_BURST_*`, events `prose_burst_fired` / `prose_burst_clean_exit`).
  It could only see a step with ZERO tool calls, and such a step leaves the
  loop via `if not tool_calls: break` — the only way past that break is a
  later-added continue (truncated/empty-turn retry, the `min_tool_calls`
  reprompt), so it was reachable solely as an interaction with those, under
  its own opt-in flag. 0 `prose_burst_fired` events in 3,240 recorded runs.
  Default path unaffected (it was opt-in).
- **Top-of-step `expects_zero_calls` spec reprompt** (v1.7). Unreachable
  since v1.8's PRE-DISPATCH gate: whenever a spec holds an
  `expects_zero_calls` requirement the gate `continue`s before
  `dispatch_tool`, so `actual_tool_calls` stays empty and the block's
  `if spec is not None and actual_tool_calls` never held for such a spec. For
  every other spec (BFCL `min_tool_calls`) it still ran `spec_validate` each
  step and then discarded every result by kind — pure wasted work, removed
  with no request-byte change (golden `spec_min_calls` / `spec_zero_calls`).
  The `min_tool_calls` reprompt at the would-be exit is unchanged.
- **Respond terminal tool** (`LUXE_RESPOND_TERMINAL`, forge-hybrid Phase 3
  B, pinned 2026-05-28 opt-in): `tools/respond.py`, the loop's pre-dispatch
  intercept with its 4 watchdog gates (compaction-phantom, early-respond,
  no-writes-late, passive-surrender; the `_RESPOND_*` constants, several of
  which the loop never even read), the first/last-write-step bookkeeping only
  the gates consumed, the `swebench_bugfix_respond` prompt variant +
  `swebench_strict_respond_only` overlay, `configs/single_64gb_swebench_respond.yaml`,
  and the swebench runner's `--respond-terminal`. REFUTED: the champion called
  it 0/14 organically and 0/14 under explicit prompt guidance (lessons.md,
  forge-hybrid closeout). The two exploratory spikes that used it as their
  only termination mechanism (`benchmarks/{gsm8k,codeneedle}/agentic_run.py`,
  commit 57d3f6d, undocumented and untested) were removed with it; recover
  from git if that line of work reopens. Its tool was never in the default
  surface, so default-flag requests are unchanged.
- **`ToolCache` + the `CACHEABLE` sets** (`tools/base.py`, and one set per
  tool module). No caller ever constructed a cache, so `dispatch_tool` never
  memoized. The per-module sets and `_build_full_tool_surface`'s third return
  value are gone (it returns `(defs, fns)`); `dispatch_tool` lost its
  `cache`/`cacheable` parameters. **`run_agent` still ACCEPTS `cache=` and
  `cacheable=`, and `run_single` `cache=`, as ignored deprecated keywords** —
  chat.sdd freezes `run_agent`'s signature for its callers, and that rule is
  about caller compatibility, so the keywords stay while the plumbing goes.
  `ToolCall.cached` stays (the `tool_call` event reports it; only the dedup
  record sets it).

**Reviewed and deliberately KEPT (2026-09): the adaptive-policy modulation
pipeline.** `compute_intervention_bias` pins `write_pressure`/`early_bail` at
0.0, so under `LUXE_ADAPTIVE_POLICY=1` their modulations are exactly 1.0 every
step and `WritePressureGuard`'s adaptive `effective_min_step` always equals the
static 5 — that half is numerically dead. It was NOT collapsed: the
`soft_anchor` bias is live, and its slew-rate-limited modulation is emitted on
every `adaptive_state` record (golden `guards_static_habituation` /
`guards_soft_anchor_diffuse` record 0.7 / 1.0 / 1.25), so the
bias -> modulation -> slew pipeline has to run anyway, and the Stage 3
invariants (bias-not-lock, slew-rate, disable-equivalence) are written against
it. Special-casing two of its three keys would save a few lines at the cost of
the one shape those invariants describe.

### Removed mechanisms (2026-10-22 refuted-lever cleanup)

Same rules as the 2026-09 record above: each was off by default, set by no
benchmark config, and refuted or inert on its own bench; the golden request
and trajectory snapshots pass unchanged (a snapshot that existed only to pin
a removed lever is deleted and named here). `outcomes.py` / the taxonomy
script keep whatever historical event names they already parse. Do not
reintroduce any of them without new evidence.

- **Post-write idle repeat counting** (`LUXE_POST_WRITE_IDLE_REPEATS`,
  `RunFlags.post_write_idle_repeats`, pinned 2026-08-10). Counted a
  post-write REPEAT call (same tool + arguments) toward the
  `post_write_idle_tools` streak, closing the `read_file`
  `_DEDUP_EXEMPT_TOOLS` blind spot in `PostWriteIdleExitGuard`. REFUTED by two
  3-rep x 10 maintain_suite A/Bs from opposite starting conditions: 27/30 and
  30/30 in BOTH arms, `post_write_idle_exit` counts identical, wall identical
  (`acceptance/pwir_ab_2026_08_10/REPORT.md`,
  `acceptance/pwir_rerun_2026_08_10/REPORT.md`; SWE-bench ruled out as a
  venue in `SWEBENCH-VENUE-CHECK.md`). Where the opportunity existed the guard
  already armed via the zero-byte path. The blind spot itself stands: a
  repeated `read_file` after a write returns content and resets the streak.
  `variants_pwir*.yaml` and `tests/test_loop_post_write_repeats.py` went with
  it (recover from history).

- **Repeat-read stub** (`LUXE_REPEAT_READ_STUB`, `RunFlags.repeat_read_stub`,
  `guardrails.repeat_read_stub_message`, `LoopState.read_results`, event
  `repeat_read_stub`, pinned 2026-10-17). Answered a `read_file` repeated
  with identical arguments and no write since with a short pointer back to
  the earlier result, provided that result was still intact in `messages`.
  INERT on its target model (`acceptance/tuning_2026_10/REPORT.md` §7):
  muse-glimmer-30B 2/6 REAL, and it fired once in 6 runs, not in either pass
  — Glimmer's re-reads come after compaction has rewritten the earlier
  result, which the stub declines by design. With it went the loop's
  `call_is_repeat` local, which had no other reader once the post-write
  repeat counting above was gone.

- **Re-read note** (`LUXE_REREAD_NOTE`, `RunFlags.reread_note`,
  `guardrails.reread_note_message` / `read_result_line_range` /
  `range_is_covered`, `LoopState.read_coverage` / `read_counts`, event
  `reread_note`, pinned 2026-10-18). Appended a note to a `read_file` result
  whose every line had already been returned since the last write. REFUTED on
  its target model (`acceptance/tuning_2026_10/REPORT.md` §10): the
  line-coverage key did catch muse-glimmer-30B's loop (10–12 notes per
  failing run) and Glimmer re-read the same lines right after each note —
  2/6 REAL, the same as the stub arm. With the prompt appendix, the
  write-pressure notice, the stub and this note all ignored, no in-loop text
  lever moves Glimmer.

### Consecutive-repeat abort is a RoleConfig switch (2026-10-15)

- `RoleConfig.stuck_loop_abort` (default **True**) gates whether
  `ConsecutiveRepeatGuard` may abort the run. Every YAML-loaded role keeps
  True, so benchmark/maintain are byte-identical (golden trajectories pass
  unchanged). Only `luxe chat`'s `/loopguard off` sets False, per turn.
- When False and the guard WOULD fire, the loop emits `stuck_loop_suppressed`
  (step, consecutive_repeat_steps) and continues; dedup substitution and the
  repeat counter are untouched, and `max_steps` remains the bound. The
  threshold `_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_REPEAT_STEPS` stays in `guardrails.py`.

### Prefix-cache accounting + the two cache experiments (2026-10-15)

Evidence: oMLX's own logs on m1 (Qwen3.6 is a hybrid model, so its prefix
cache resumes only at boundary snapshots: every 4,096 tokens plus the tail of
each prompt). Within a run, step N+1 reuses step N's whole prompt. Two things
break that:
(a) the model's reasoning is dropped from history, so the template renders an
empty `<think>` for every earlier step instead of what the model actually
generated; (b) TieredCompact rewrites a message just upstream of the
`keep_recent` window on EVERY step once pressure passes phase 1, and phase 1
re-truncates its own output once more (`[Truncated — 33 chars removed]`, a
false count). Each rewrite forces everything after it to be prefilled again.

- `step_usage` event (ADDITIVE, ungated like the tool-call events): per step
  `prompt_tokens`, `cached_tokens`, `completion_tokens`, `prefill_s`,
  `decode_s`, `reasoning_chars` — the server's numbers (oMLX `usage` /
  llama-server `timings`, `backend._server_perf`), None when unreported. No
  wall-clock fields: golden trajectories pin these records.
- `AgentResult.cached_tokens / perf_prompt_tokens / prefill_s / prefill_tokens
  / decode_s / decode_tokens / first_step_*`: reporting only; no loop logic
  reads them (chat's `pp · tg` and `cache N%` chips do).
- `LUXE_REASONING_ROUNDTRIP=1` (opt-in, default OFF = byte-identical): every
  assistant message the loop appends carries `reasoning_content` when the
  server split reasoning off. **Benched, NOT promoted (2026-10-15):** −28%
  wall via −32% completion tokens, but it changes the trajectory (29/30, one
  write-avoidant `document-config`, a THIN architecture doc). Needs a wider
  run (RC vs C) before any default change.
- `LUXE_COMPACT_CACHE_FRIENDLY` — **default ON since 2026-10-15**; only the
  exact string `0` restores the legacy (re-truncating) phase 1. Phase 1
  never re-truncates a result already carrying its marker, and below
  phase-2 pressure it waits until `CACHE_FRIENDLY_BATCH` (4) eligible
  results are pending, so the prefix is rewritten once per batch instead of
  once per step. Phases 2/3 unchanged. Benched 30/30 (= base), fires 66→27,
  prefill −16%, trajectories identical to legacy wherever compaction never
  fires. Golden trajectories did not move: their scenarios compact at
  phase 2/3 with tool results under the truncation size, so phase 1 never
  had anything to re-truncate. `TieredCompact(cache_friendly=False)` stays
  the constructor default (the loop passes the flag); don't flip that too.
- Bench verdicts for both: `acceptance/cache_ab_2026_10/REPORT.md`.
- `run_agent(prior_messages=)` (2026-10-16, chat-only turn carry — chat.sdd):
  the run starts from those messages plus `task_prompt` as a new user
  message. `prior_messages[0]` must equal this run's system message or the
  carry is ignored (`turn_carry_rejected` event, fresh start); a used carry
  emits `turn_carry` and sets `AgentResult.carried`. `AgentResult.messages`
  is the conversation as last sent (post-compaction) plus the final answer
  on a clean finish — additive, read only by chat. None/unused on every
  benchmark/maintain call: their message list is built exactly as before.

## Must not (reflect)

- Encode benchmark semantics in verifier prompts (e.g. "a tool call was warranted") —
  evaluator-shaped phrasing is forbidden (anti-overfitting). The multi_turn verifier asks
  about the *request being carried out*, never about tool calls.
- Let verify-only mutate the generation transcript, or let Phase 2 repair invoke tools
  outside the original trajectory's tool surface (pre-registered repair budget).

### Stage 3 / v1.11 adaptive-policy invariants (pinned 2026-05-20)

- **score_log ownership**: `loop.py` owns the per-step convergence-score deque; `convergence.py` (`compute_convergence_score`) stays stateless. Composition boundary is the `_compute_within_run_state(score_log, tool_history, step)` signature — loop.py is the sole writer, convergence.py is the sole consumer
- **Bias-not-lock**: no signal combination may reduce intervention probability to zero or increase it above the configured ceiling. Adaptive policy modulates intervention timing/intensity, never enables/disables. Property-tested in Phase 1
- **Slew-rate limit**: per-step intensity-modifier delta bounded by `LUXE_ADAPTIVE_MAX_INTENSITY_DELTA_PER_STEP` (default 0.3). Prevents oscillation; makes regressions smoother instead of catastrophic
- **Disable-equivalence**: `LUXE_ADAPTIVE_POLICY=0` (or unset) produces byte-identical behavior to v1.10.5
- **Priors are log-only in v1.11**: `LUXE_LOAD_PRIORS=1` reads `~/.luxe/cohort-history/<instance>.json` and logs to events.jsonl, but does **not** influence intervention intensity (deferred to v1.11.1+)

### Stage 3 / v1.11 Phase B activation — TRIED + REVERTED (2026-05-21)

- **no_write bias RETIRED (KEPT)**: the no_write → write_pressure/early_bail bias is pinned at 0.0. Phase A (`project_v111_phaseA_calibration`) proved no_write non-selective (precision ≤31% — read-heavy successes hit the same depths as stalls). This is behaviorally v1.10.5-neutral (write_pressure reverts to its static schedule) and stays.
- **score_trend → soft_anchor collapse promotion: REVERTED**. The Phase B lever raised the score<LOW band response one rung (breadth_probe → soft_anchor commitment nudge) on the confirmed-collapse signal `conv<LOW AND step>=_COLLAPSE_MIN_STEP AND trend<=0`. Phase D (n=75 ×3) showed it **net-negative**: it fired 3/3 on xarray-3305 (strong→plausible) and pylint-4661 (plausible→wrong_target), demoting tier via PREMATURE COMMITMENT, with 0 conversions and 0 Docker gain. cohort_shift_3x3 = 3 deterministic losses → HOLD. The loop.py consumer was reverted; the bias is retained as observability only.
- **Root-cause lesson for v1.11.1**: the step-`_COLLAPSE_MIN_STEP` collapse signature cannot distinguish "truly stalled" from "mid-deep-dive with a transient low-trend dip", so the commitment nudge derails recovering trajectories. The `trend<=0` Pareto guard was necessary but NOT sufficient. A usable stall signal needs non-recovery specificity (sustained trend<=0 over K steps, or a semantic-breadth-saturation signal — the "breadth not temporal counters" direction) — NOT a single-step snapshot.
- The "intensity-not-enable" framing (selecting a band-response message variant for an already-triggered early_bail predicate) remains a valid reading of the bias-only Must-not, should a future lever reuse the promotion mechanism with a better gate.

## Must not
- Inline prompt strings in `single.py` or `cli.py` (un-couples variants from runtime)
- Add feature flags or backwards-compatibility shims for retired modes (swarm / micro / phased)
- Add a new prompt variant without a corresponding test in `tests/test_prompts.py`
- (v1.11) Mutate the score_log from `convergence.py` — ownership lives in `loop.py`
- (v1.11) Let a Stage 3 signal hard-gate (enable/disable) an intervention dispatch — bias only
- (v1.11) Wire cross-cycle priors into intervention intensity computation — log-only this cycle
- (v1.11.1+) Ship `intervention_history_within_run` without capping its weight below all other Stage 3 signals AND restricting it to suppress-only (never amplify) — prevents intervention-aversion attractor

